        'keyword'    => '2026 federal tax brackets',
        'desc'       => 'The 2026 federal tax brackets run from 10% to 37%. See every bracket by filing status, the standard deduction, and a worked example.',
        'excerpt'    => 'All seven 2026 federal tax brackets, for every filing status, with the standard deduction and a step-by-step worked example.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-08 09:00',
        'answer'     => 'The 2026 federal income tax has seven brackets: 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35% and 37%. For a single filer, the 10% bracket covers income up to $12,400, then each bracket applies only to the income within that range — not your whole income. The 2026 standard deduction is $16,100 for single filers, $32,200 for married filing jointly, and $24,150 for head of household.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'The 2026 federal brackets are 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35% and 37%, and they are marginal — only income within each bracket is taxed at that rate.',
            'The 2026 standard deduction is $16,100 single, $32,200 married filing jointly, and $24,150 head of household.',
            'Your "tax bracket" (marginal rate) and your actual effective tax rate (total tax ÷ total income) are almost always different numbers — effective rate is always lower.',
            'These figures come from IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32 and are the same numbers used throughout this site\'s calculators.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If you are looking up the <strong>2026 federal tax brackets</strong>, here is the short version: there are seven brackets, from 10% up to 37%, and they are marginal, meaning each rate only applies to the slice of income that falls inside that bracket, not your entire income. This guide lists every bracket for all four filing statuses, explains how the math actually works with a full worked example, and covers the standard deduction you subtract before any of this applies. Run your own numbers in our <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a>.</p>'
            . '<h2>2026 tax brackets: single filers</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Taxable income range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10%</td><td>$0 to $12,400</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>$12,400 to $50,400</td></tr><tr><td>22%</td><td>$50,400 to $105,700</td></tr><tr><td>24%</td><td>$105,700 to $201,775</td></tr><tr><td>32%</td><td>$201,775 to $256,225</td></tr><tr><td>35%</td><td>$256,225 to $640,600</td></tr><tr><td>37%</td><td>Above $640,600</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>2026 tax brackets: married filing jointly</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Taxable income range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10%</td><td>$0 to $24,800</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>$24,800 to $100,800</td></tr><tr><td>22%</td><td>$100,800 to $211,400</td></tr><tr><td>24%</td><td>$211,400 to $403,550</td></tr><tr><td>32%</td><td>$403,550 to $512,450</td></tr><tr><td>35%</td><td>$512,450 to $768,700</td></tr><tr><td>37%</td><td>Above $768,700</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>2026 tax brackets: head of household</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Taxable income range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10%</td><td>$0 to $17,700</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>$17,700 to $67,450</td></tr><tr><td>22%</td><td>$67,450 to $105,700</td></tr><tr><td>24%</td><td>$105,700 to $201,750</td></tr><tr><td>32%</td><td>$201,750 to $256,200</td></tr><tr><td>35%</td><td>$256,200 to $640,600</td></tr><tr><td>37%</td><td>Above $640,600</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>2026 tax brackets: married filing separately</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Rate</th><th>Taxable income range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10%</td><td>$0 to $12,400</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>$12,400 to $50,400</td></tr><tr><td>22%</td><td>$50,400 to $105,700</td></tr><tr><td>24%</td><td>$105,700 to $201,775</td></tr><tr><td>32%</td><td>$201,775 to $256,225</td></tr><tr><td>35%</td><td>$256,225 to $384,350</td></tr><tr><td>37%</td><td>Above $384,350</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>The 2026 standard deduction</h2><p>Before any bracket applies, most filers subtract the standard deduction from their gross income to get taxable income. For 2026: <strong>$16,100</strong> single or married filing separately, <strong>$32,200</strong> married filing jointly, and <strong>$24,150</strong> head of household. Filers 65 or older, or blind, get an additional amount on top of these figures.</p><p>The standard deduction matters just as much as the brackets themselves for most filers, since it is the single biggest factor in converting your gross income into the taxable income the brackets actually apply to. A single filer earning $60,000 does not pay tax on $60,000 at these rates — after subtracting the $16,100 standard deduction, only $43,900 is taxable, meaning this filer never actually reaches the 22% bracket at all despite earning $60,000. Itemizing deductions instead — mortgage interest, charitable gifts, and SALT up to its cap — only makes sense once those itemized amounts exceed the standard deduction for your filing status.</p>'
            . '<h2>Why "marginal" brackets confuse people</h2><p>The single biggest misunderstanding about tax brackets: moving into a higher bracket does not mean your entire income gets taxed at the higher rate. Only the portion of income that actually falls inside that bracket is taxed there. A single filer with $110,000 of taxable income is technically "in the 24% bracket," but their first $12,400 is still taxed at 10%, the next chunk at 12%, and so on — only the small slice between $105,700 and $110,000 is actually taxed at 24%.</p><p>This misunderstanding leads some people to turn down a raise or a bonus out of fear it will "push them into a higher bracket and leave them with less money overall." That is mathematically impossible under a marginal system — earning one more dollar can never reduce your after-tax income, because only that specific dollar (and any others above the threshold) is taxed at the new, higher rate. The rest of your income keeps being taxed exactly as it was before.</p>'
            . '<h2>Worked example: a single filer with $110,000 taxable income</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Bracket</th><th>Income taxed at this rate</th><th>Tax owed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10%</td><td>$12,400</td><td>$1,240.00</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>$38,000 ($50,400 − $12,400)</td><td>$4,560.00</td></tr><tr><td>22%</td><td>$55,300 ($105,700 − $50,400)</td><td>$12,166.00</td></tr><tr><td>24%</td><td>$4,300 ($110,000 − $105,700)</td><td>$1,032.00</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Total tax: $1,240 + $4,560 + $12,166 + $1,032 = <strong>$18,998</strong>. That is an effective tax rate of about 17.3% ($18,998 ÷ $110,000) — noticeably lower than the 24% "bracket" this filer is technically in. This gap between marginal rate and effective rate is true for every filer; the top bracket you touch is never the rate you pay on your whole income.</p>'
            . '<h2>Marginal rate vs. effective rate: why both numbers matter</h2><p>Your <strong>marginal rate</strong> is the rate on your next dollar of income — the bracket you are "in." It is the number that matters for decisions like a year-end bonus, a Roth conversion, or extra freelance income, since that next dollar is taxed at your marginal rate. Your <strong>effective rate</strong> is your total tax divided by your total income — the number that describes your real overall tax burden. Both are useful, but for different questions. See our <a href="/year-end-tax-planning-checklist/">year-end tax planning checklist</a> for moves that are specifically about managing which bracket your next dollar lands in.</p>'
            . '<h2>How these brackets compare to recent years</h2><p>Tax brackets are adjusted for inflation every year by the IRS, which is why the dollar thresholds shift annually even when the seven rates themselves (10% through 37%) stay the same. These 2026 figures come from IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32. The 2027 brackets had not yet been published by the IRS at the time of this guide\'s writing — the IRS typically releases the following year\'s figures in the fall, so check IRS.gov for the current numbers before relying on next year\'s bracket thresholds.</p>'
            . '<h2>Using your bracket to plan, not just to file</h2><p>Knowing exactly where your bracket thresholds sit lets you make better decisions before the year ends, not just better predictions at filing time. A few common examples:</p><ul><li><strong>Bracket filling for a Roth conversion.</strong> If you have room left in a lower bracket, converting just enough traditional IRA money to use up that remaining room, without spilling into the next bracket, lets you pay tax on the conversion at a rate you already know and control. See our <a href="/roth-conversion-before-year-end/">Roth conversion before year-end guide</a> for the exact mechanics.</li><li><strong>Timing a bonus or freelance income.</strong> If a bonus or a large freelance payment could land in either December or January, knowing which bracket each year leaves you in can make a real difference, especially near a bracket boundary.</li><li><strong>Harvesting capital losses.</strong> Losses offset gains dollar for dollar regardless of bracket, but knowing your ordinary bracket helps you judge how much of a loss carryforward is actually worth harvesting this year versus banking for a higher-income year later.</li><li><strong>Deciding how much to contribute pre-tax.</strong> Additional 401(k) or traditional IRA contributions reduce your taxable income dollar for dollar, which is most valuable when that dollar would otherwise have been taxed at a higher bracket.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>How brackets have shifted over the past few years</h2><p>The seven rates themselves — 10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35% and 37% — have stayed the same since the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act took effect for 2018, and the One Big Beautiful Bill Act kept this same seven-bracket structure in place for 2026 and beyond rather than reverting to the older, higher pre-2018 rate structure that had been scheduled to return. What changes every year is purely the dollar thresholds, which the IRS adjusts upward for inflation. That is why the 2026 single-filer 22% bracket starts at $50,400 rather than a round, memorable number — it is the prior year\'s threshold compounded by that year\'s inflation adjustment, not a number Congress set directly.</p>'
            . '<h2>What this does not cover</h2><p>These are federal brackets only — many states also charge their own income tax with separate brackets (and nine states charge none at all; see our <a href="/states-with-no-income-tax/">guide to states with no income tax</a>). This guide also does not cover capital gains tax, which uses separate, generally lower rates — see our <a href="/tax-loss-harvesting/">tax loss harvesting guide</a> for how capital gains brackets work. High earners should also check whether the <a href="/net-investment-income-tax-niit-guide/">Net Investment Income Tax</a> applies on top of these ordinary brackets.</p>'
            . '<h2>A worked example for married filing jointly</h2><p>A married couple with $240,000 of taxable income:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Bracket</th><th>Income taxed at this rate</th><th>Tax owed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10%</td><td>$24,800</td><td>$2,480.00</td></tr><tr><td>12%</td><td>$76,000 ($100,800 − $24,800)</td><td>$9,120.00</td></tr><tr><td>22%</td><td>$110,600 ($211,400 − $100,800)</td><td>$24,332.00</td></tr><tr><td>24%</td><td>$28,600 ($240,000 − $211,400)</td><td>$6,864.00</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Total: $2,480 + $9,120 + $24,332 + $6,864 = <strong>$42,796</strong>, an effective rate of about 17.8% — again well below the 24% top marginal bracket this couple is technically in. Notice how the exact same logic from the single-filer example applies here, just with different thresholds for married filing jointly.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes people make</h2><ul><li><strong>Thinking a raise or bonus pushes your whole income into a higher bracket.</strong> It does not — only the income above the new threshold is taxed at the higher rate.</li><li><strong>Confusing marginal rate with effective rate.</strong> Your effective rate, the one that describes your real tax burden, is always lower than your top marginal bracket.</li><li><strong>Forgetting the standard deduction before applying brackets.</strong> Brackets apply to taxable income, after the standard deduction (or itemized deductions), not your gross income.</li><li><strong>Assuming last year\'s thresholds still apply.</strong> The IRS adjusts every bracket\'s dollar threshold annually for inflation — always confirm the current year\'s figures.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax advice. Use the <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> for a full estimate including your specific deductions and credits.</p>'
            . '<h2>A quick glossary</h2><ul><li><strong>Marginal tax rate</strong>. The rate applied to your next dollar of income — the bracket you are "in."</li><li><strong>Effective tax rate</strong>. Your total tax divided by your total income, which blends all the brackets your income actually passed through.</li><li><strong>Taxable income</strong>. Your income after subtracting the standard deduction (or itemized deductions) — the number brackets actually apply to, not your gross income.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>IRS Revenue Procedure 2025-32 (2026 inflation adjustments); IRS <a href="https://www.irs.gov/filing/federal-income-tax-rates-and-brackets" rel="noopener" target="_blank">federal income tax rates and brackets</a>.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'What are the 2026 federal tax brackets?', '10%, 12%, 22%, 24%, 32%, 35% and 37%. The exact dollar ranges differ by filing status — see the tables above for single, married filing jointly, head of household, and married filing separately.' ],
            [ 'What is the standard deduction for 2026?', '$16,100 for single filers and married filing separately, $32,200 for married filing jointly, and $24,150 for head of household. Filers 65 or older, or blind, receive an additional amount.' ],
            [ 'Does moving into a higher tax bracket mean all my income is taxed at that rate?', 'No. U.S. federal tax brackets are marginal — only the portion of your income that falls within a given bracket is taxed at that bracket\'s rate. The rest of your income is still taxed at the lower brackets below it.' ],
            [ 'What is the difference between marginal and effective tax rate?', 'Your marginal rate is the rate on your next dollar of income — the bracket you are technically "in." Your effective rate is your total tax divided by your total income, and it is always lower than your top marginal bracket because of how marginal brackets stack.' ],
            [ 'Have the 2027 tax brackets been announced?', 'Not as of this guide\'s writing. The IRS typically releases the next year\'s inflation-adjusted brackets in the fall. Check IRS.gov for the current 2027 figures once published.' ],
