        ],
    ],
    [
        'slug'       => 'states-with-no-income-tax',
        'title'      => '9 States With No Income Tax in 2026',
        'keyword'    => 'states with no state tax',
        'desc'       => 'Nine states charge no state income tax in 2026: Alaska, Florida, Nevada, New Hampshire, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington and Wyoming.',
        'excerpt'    => 'All nine no-income-tax states, the catches each one has, and how to tell if moving there would actually save you money.',
        'publish_at' => '2026-11-08 21:00',
        'answer'     => 'Nine states charge no state income tax in 2026: Alaska, Florida, Nevada, New Hampshire, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington and Wyoming. "No income tax" does not mean no state taxes at all — these states typically make up the revenue through higher property tax, sales tax, or other sources, so the real savings depend on your income level and spending habits, not just the headline fact.',
        'takeaways'  => [
            'Nine states have no state income tax: Alaska, Florida, Nevada, New Hampshire, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington and Wyoming.',
            'Washington has no wage income tax but does tax large long-term capital gains above a threshold.',
            'New Hampshire fully repealed its old tax on interest and dividends starting January 1, 2025, so it now has zero state income tax of any kind.',
            'No-income-tax states usually offset the lost revenue with higher property tax or sales tax — the real savings depend on your income and spending, not just the state name.',
        ],
        'content'    => '<p class="lead">If you are checking which <strong>states with no state tax</strong> exist, the list is short and has not changed in years: nine states charge zero state income tax. But "no income tax" is only half the picture — every state needs revenue from somewhere, so these nine states typically lean harder on property tax, sales tax, or other sources instead. This guide lists all nine, explains the real catches in a few of them, and walks through how to actually estimate whether living in one would save you money.</p>'
            . '<h2>The 9 states with no income tax in 2026</h2><table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>Notable catch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Alaska</td><td>No sales tax either, but cost of living runs above the national average</td></tr><tr><td>Florida</td><td>Relies heavily on sales tax and tourism-related revenue</td></tr><tr><td>Nevada</td><td>Relies heavily on sales tax and gaming/tourism revenue</td></tr><tr><td>New Hampshire</td><td>Fully repealed its interest/dividends tax starting 2025; relatively high property tax</td></tr><tr><td>South Dakota</td><td>Relies on sales tax; low overall tax burden</td></tr><tr><td>Tennessee</td><td>One of the highest combined state/local sales tax rates in the country</td></tr><tr><td>Texas</td><td>No income tax, but some of the highest property tax rates in the country</td></tr><tr><td>Washington</td><td>No wage income tax, but taxes large long-term capital gains above a threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Wyoming</td><td>Low overall tax burden; relies partly on mineral/energy revenue</td></tr></tbody></table>'
            . '<h2>New Hampshire: the most recent change</h2><p>New Hampshire used to be a partial exception on this list — it taxed interest and dividend income even though it never taxed wages. That tax was fully repealed effective January 1, 2025, so New Hampshire is now a complete no-income-tax state with no exceptions, matching the other eight on this list.</p>'
            . '<h2>Washington: the "no income tax" state that taxes some income</h2><p>Washington has no tax on wages or ordinary income, but it does apply a tax to long-term capital gains above an inflation-adjusted threshold (roughly $270,000 of gains in recent years, adjusted annually). A law signed in 2026 adds a further, higher tier on capital gains income above $1 million starting in 2028. For most residents with no large investment sales, Washington functions as a true no-income-tax state in practice — but a resident selling significant investments or a business should check the current capital gains threshold before assuming zero state tax applies.</p>'
            . '<h2>Alaska: no income tax and no sales tax, but a cost-of-living catch</h2><p>Alaska is the only state on this list with neither an income tax nor a state sales tax (some local sales taxes exist at the municipal level). That sounds like the best of both worlds, and in pure tax terms it is — but multiple cost-of-living surveys consistently show Alaska\'s overall cost of living running well above the national average, driven by housing, groceries, and energy costs in many areas. The tax savings are real, but they can be partly or fully offset by higher day-to-day costs depending on where in the state you live. Alaska also uniquely pays residents an annual dividend from state oil revenue (the Permanent Fund Dividend), which partially offsets living costs for full-year residents — a benefit no other state on this list offers.</p>'
            . '<h2>The trade-off: how these states make up the revenue</h2><ul><li><strong>Texas</strong> has some of the highest effective property tax rates in the country — a homeowner can pay more in property tax than they would have paid in income tax in many other states.</li><li><strong>Tennessee</strong> has one of the highest combined state and local sales tax rates nationally, which matters more for lower earners who spend a larger share of their income.</li><li><strong>Florida and Nevada</strong> lean heavily on sales tax and tourism-related revenue (hotel taxes, gaming revenue) rather than taxing residents\' income or property as heavily.</li><li><strong>Wyoming</strong> benefits from mineral and energy extraction revenue, which reduces the pressure to raise other taxes.</li></ul><p>The practical lesson: "no income tax" tells you about one line item, not your total tax burden. A high earner with a modest home and modest spending often does best in a no-income-tax state. A homeowner with a large, high-value property in Texas, or a lower earner doing most of their spending in Tennessee, may end up paying a comparable or even higher total tax burden than they would in a state with an income tax but lower property or sales tax rates.</p>'
            . '<h2>A side-by-side comparison worth running yourself</h2><p>The honest way to compare a no-income-tax state against your current one is not to look at the income tax line alone — it is to estimate your total state and local tax bill in both places. Start with what you would actually pay in state income tax where you live now (use our <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> to estimate your federal number, then check your current state\'s own bracket table for the state portion). Then estimate property tax on a comparable home in the destination state, and a rough annual sales tax figure based on your typical spending. Add those two destination-state numbers together and compare the total against what you currently pay. For many W-2 earners with moderate spending, the no-income-tax state still wins clearly. For someone buying an expensive home in Texas, or doing most of their spending in Tennessee, the gap can shrink substantially or even reverse.</p>'
            . '<h2>Who benefits the most from living in a no-income-tax state</h2><ul><li><strong>High W-2 earners</strong> with relatively modest housing and spending see the clearest, most direct savings, since income tax scales with income while property and sales tax do not scale the same way.</li><li><strong>Retirees drawing from investments or <a href="/tool/401k-early-withdrawal-calculator/">401(k) withdrawals</a></strong> avoid state tax on that income entirely in most of these states (Washington\'s capital gains tax being the one partial exception for very large gains).</li><li><strong>Remote workers who can choose where to live</strong> get to capture the full benefit without changing jobs, assuming their employer correctly withholds (or stops withholding) state tax for their new state of residence.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Before you move for the tax savings</h2><p>Run the full comparison, not just the income tax line: look up the destination state\'s average property tax rate on a home at your target price point, its state and local sales tax rate, and any other state-specific fees or taxes that might apply to your situation. Our <a href="/tool/property-tax-calculator">property tax calculator</a> and <a href="{tool:income-tax-calculator}">income tax calculator</a> can help build out both sides of that comparison before you decide a move is worth it purely for the tax savings.</p>'
            . '<h2>Common mistakes people make</h2><ul><li><strong>Assuming "no income tax" means low overall taxes.</strong> Texas and New Hampshire both have above-average property tax; Tennessee has above-average sales tax.</li><li><strong>Forgetting Washington\'s capital gains tax applies to large investment sales.</strong> It is not a pure no-income-tax state for every situation.</li><li><strong>Not accounting for cost of living, not just tax rates.</strong> Alaska\'s tax advantages can be partly offset by a higher cost of living in many areas.</li><li><strong>Moving without updating withholding correctly.</strong> A remote worker who moves needs their employer to correctly stop state withholding for the old state and handle the new state properly.</li></ul>'
            . '<p>This guide is educational and not personal tax or relocation advice. State tax rules change; confirm current rates with each state\'s department of revenue before making a decision based on tax savings alone.</p>'
            . '<h2>A quick glossary</h2><ul><li><strong>Income tax</strong>. A tax on wages, salary and other earned income, charged by both the federal government and, in 41 states, the state government as well.</li><li><strong>Capital gains tax</strong>. A separate tax on profit from selling an investment or asset — Washington is the one state on this list that taxes large long-term capital gains despite having no wage income tax.</li><li><strong>Effective tax burden</strong>. The real, total share of your income or spending that goes to state and local taxes combined — income tax, property tax and sales tax together — which is the number that actually matters when comparing states.</li></ul>'
            . '<h2>Sources</h2><p>Washington State Department of Revenue, <a href="https://dor.wa.gov/taxes-rates/other-taxes/capital-gains-tax" rel="noopener" target="_blank">capital gains tax</a>; New Hampshire Department of Revenue Administration, <a href="https://www.revenue.nh.gov/resource-center/frequently-asked-questions/interest-dividends-tax-faq" rel="noopener" target="_blank">interest and dividends tax</a> (repealed effective January 1, 2025); Tax Foundation, <a href="https://taxfoundation.org/data/all/state/state-income-tax-rates/" rel="noopener" target="_blank">state individual income tax rates</a>.</p>'
            . '{faq}',
        'faqs'       => [
            [ 'Which states have no state income tax?', 'Nine states: Alaska, Florida, Nevada, New Hampshire, South Dakota, Tennessee, Texas, Washington and Wyoming. This list has been stable for several years, with New Hampshire\'s full repeal of its interest/dividends tax in 2025 being the most recent change.' ],
            [ 'Does Washington really have no income tax?', 'Washington has no tax on wages or ordinary income, but it does tax large long-term capital gains above a threshold (roughly $270,000, adjusted annually). For most residents without major investment sales, it functions as a no-income-tax state in practice.' ],
            [ 'Is it cheaper to live in a state with no income tax?', 'Not necessarily. These states typically make up the revenue through higher property tax (Texas, New Hampshire), higher sales tax (Tennessee), or tourism/resource revenue (Florida, Nevada, Wyoming, Alaska). Your total tax burden depends on your income, home value, and spending, not just the absence of income tax.' ],
            [ 'Does New Hampshire tax any income at all?', 'As of January 1, 2025, no. New Hampshire fully repealed its previous tax on interest and dividend income, which had been the one exception to its no-income-tax status. It now has zero state income tax of any kind.' ],
            [ 'Which no-income-tax state has the lowest overall tax burden?', 'It varies by individual circumstances, but Wyoming and South Dakota are frequently cited as having among the lowest overall state and local tax burdens of the nine, partly due to lower property and sales tax rates compared with Texas or Tennessee.' ],
